# 2026-10-11: Harden the request boundary

## Why it changed

Requests reached the service without one consistent validation boundary. Each handler decoded its own fields, returned
a different error shape, and logged different context. That made malformed input difficult to diagnose and made a new
route copy behavior that was already drifting.

This work puts validation before the application service so handlers receive one trusted shape and callers receive one
documented failure contract.

## What changed

The request model now owns field constraints and normalization. Middleware parses the request once, rejects unsupported
content types, and returns the shared error envelope before calling the service. Existing handlers consume the parsed
model instead of reading the raw request independently.

Tests cover missing, malformed and normalized values as well as the unchanged success path. The interface guide records
the response status, error code and payload so callers do not have to infer the contract from tests.

## Decisions

- Validation stays at the transport boundary; domain rules remain in the service layer.
- Invalid input returns one stable error code and never includes the rejected value in logs.
- Existing valid requests keep their response shape, so the change requires no caller migration.

Alternatives considered were leaving validation in each handler or moving transport parsing into the service. The first
keeps the drift, while the second couples domain behavior to HTTP.

## Verification

The completed review checked:

- formatting and static analysis;
- focused request-validation tests;
- the complete unit test suite;
- a local valid request and representative invalid requests; and
- the interface documentation against the implemented response.

Record commands and results here when they are important evidence. Do not claim a deployed check unless it actually ran
against the applicable environment.

## Follow-up

None for this example. Real records keep unresolved work here with an issue, plan item or decision owner rather than
silently presenting partial work as complete.
